/* Generated by tools/build.js from data/cissp.js. Edit that file, not this one. */
CertHub.addQuestions("cissp", [["cs1",0,1,"A security manager discovers that a proposed marketing initiative will create risk above the organization's stated risk appetite. What should the manager do FIRST?",["Present the risk and options to senior management so the business owner can make an informed decision","Block the initiative until controls reduce the risk","Accept the risk on behalf of the organization","Purchase cyber insurance to transfer the risk"],0,"Security advises; senior management owns risk decisions. Informing them with options is the manager's role.","Outline 1.10",[null,"Blocking the initiative oversteps the security role; security advises on risk, but the business owner decides whether a business initiative proceeds.","A security manager does not have the authority to accept risk above the stated appetite on the organization's behalf; that decision belongs to senior management.","Insurance is one possible response, but choosing it is a management decision made after the risk is presented, not the manager's first step."],null],["cs2",0,1,"Under the ISC2 Code of Ethics, which canon takes precedence when canons conflict?",["Provide diligent and competent service to principals","Protect society, the common good, necessary public trust and confidence, and the infrastructure","Advance and protect the profession","Act honorably, honestly, justly, responsibly and legally"],1,"The canons are in priority order, and protecting society comes first.","Outline 1.1",["Service to principals is the third canon, so it yields to protecting society and to acting honorably when they conflict.",null,"Advancing and protecting the profession is the fourth and lowest-priority canon.","Acting honorably, honestly, justly, responsibly and legally is the second canon; it ranks below protecting society."],null],["cs3",0,1,"An asset worth $200,000 has an exposure factor of 25% and is expected to be hit once every four years. What is the annualized loss expectancy?",["$50,000","$200,000","$12,500","$800,000"],2,"SLE = 200,000 x 0.25 = 50,000; ARO = 0.25; ALE = 50,000 x 0.25 = 12,500.","Outline 1.10",["$50,000 is the single loss expectancy (200,000 x 0.25); it still has to be multiplied by the ARO of 0.25 to get the ALE.","$200,000 is the asset value; it ignores both the 25% exposure factor and the once-in-four-years rate.",null,"$800,000 comes from multiplying by 4 instead of by the ARO of 0.25 (one in four years)."],null],["cs4",0,1,"Which activity BEST demonstrates due diligence before signing a contract with a new cloud provider?",["Encrypting data after migration","Signing the contract with a standard SLA","Training staff on the new service","Reviewing the provider's SOC 2 Type II report and security practices"],3,"Due diligence is researching and understanding risk before acting; reviewing assurance reports does that.","Outline 1.3",["Encrypting data after migration is a protective control (due care) applied after the decision, not research done before signing.","Signing with a standard SLA commits to the provider without first investigating its security, which is the opposite of due diligence.","Staff training on the service happens after adoption and addresses usage, not the provider's risk before contracting.",null],null],["cs5",0,1,"When developing a business continuity plan, what should be done FIRST?",["Obtain senior management support and define the scope","Conduct a business impact analysis","Select an alternate processing site","Test the recovery procedures"],0,"Without management backing and a defined scope, the BIA and later steps lack authority and resources.","Outline 1.8",[null,"The BIA is a core early step, but it needs management's mandate and a defined scope first to have authority and resources.","An alternate site is a recovery strategy chosen after the BIA sets RTOs, so it comes much later in planning.","Testing recovery procedures is one of the last steps; there is nothing to test until the plan has been written."],null],["cs6",0,1,"Which document is MANDATORY and specifies a particular technology or configuration required across the organization?",["Guideline","Standard","Policy","Baseline recommendation"],1,"Standards are mandatory and specific; policies are high level and guidelines are optional advice.","Outline 1.7",["A guideline is optional, recommended advice, not a mandatory requirement.",null,"A policy is mandatory but high level; it states management intent without naming specific technologies or settings.","A baseline sets minimum configuration for a system type, and calling it a 'recommendation' makes it optional, which contradicts the question."],null],["cs7",0,1,"The maximum amount of time a business process can be unavailable before the organization suffers unacceptable harm is called the:",["Recovery point objective (RPO)","Mean time to repair (MTTR)","Maximum tolerable downtime (MTD)","Work recovery time (WRT)"],2,"MTD is the upper limit; RTO must be set below it.","Outline 1.8",["RPO is the maximum acceptable data loss measured back in time, not how long a process can be down.","MTTR is the average time to repair a component, an operational metric rather than a business tolerance limit.",null,"Work recovery time is the time needed after systems are restored to verify data and resume work; it is one part of the total downtime."],null],["cs8",0,1,"An organization buys cyber insurance to cover losses from a data breach. Which risk response is this?",["Avoidance","Mitigation","Acceptance","Transference"],3,"Insurance shifts the financial impact to a third party, which is risk transfer.","Outline 1.10",["Avoidance means stopping the risky activity altogether; buying insurance keeps the activity running.","Mitigation reduces the likelihood or impact with controls; insurance does not make a breach less likely or less damaging.","Acceptance means knowingly bearing the loss yourself, whereas insurance moves the financial loss to the insurer.",null],null],["cs9",0,1,"Which threat modeling method categorizes threats as spoofing, tampering, repudiation, information disclosure, denial of service and elevation of privilege?",["STRIDE","PASTA","DREAD","OCTAVE"],0,"STRIDE is Microsoft's threat categorization model.","Outline 1.11",[null,"PASTA is a seven-stage, risk-centric threat modeling process, not a six-category threat classification.","DREAD rates threats by damage, reproducibility, exploitability, affected users and discoverability; it scores threats rather than categorizing them.","OCTAVE is an organizational risk assessment methodology from Carnegie Mellon, not a threat category mnemonic."],null],["cs10",0,1,"What is the BEST way to reduce the risk of fraud by an employee who controls both vendor creation and payment approval?",["Increase the employee's background check depth","Separate those duties between different employees","Require annual ethics training","Install cameras in the accounting office"],1,"Separation of duties prevents one person from completing a fraudulent transaction alone.","Outline 1.9",["Deeper background checks screen people at hiring but do not stop one trusted person from both creating and paying a fake vendor.",null,"Ethics training raises awareness but is not a control that prevents a single person from completing the fraud.","Cameras can deter physical misconduct but cannot see or stop fraudulent vendor records created in a system."],null],["cs11",0,1,"A company wants to ensure its security program supports the organization's goals. What is MOST important?",["Adopting the latest security technologies","Maximizing the number of controls implemented","Aligning security governance with business strategy, goals and mission","Matching the security budget of competitors"],2,"Governance exists to support business objectives, not technology for its own sake.","Outline 1.3",["Adopting new technology for its own sake does not ensure the program supports what the business needs.","More controls is not better; excess controls waste money and can hinder the business without reducing relevant risk.",null,"Competitors' budgets reflect their risks and goals, not yours, so matching them does not align security with your strategy."],null],["cs12",0,1,"Personal data is being transferred from the EU to a processor in another country. What should the security professional ensure FIRST?",["That the data is encrypted with AES-256","That the processor has a firewall","That the transfer is logged in the SIEM","That a lawful transfer mechanism, such as an adequacy decision or standard contractual clauses, is in place"],3,"Transborder data flow is a legal requirement first; technical controls come after legal compliance.","Outline 1.4",["AES-256 protects confidentiality, but encryption alone does not make an international transfer lawful under GDPR.","A firewall at the processor is a technical control; the legal basis for the transfer must be established first.","Logging the transfer records that it happened but does nothing to make it compliant.",null],null],["cs13",0,1,"Which is the MOST important measure of a security awareness program's effectiveness?",["A reduction in security incidents caused by user behavior","Percentage of staff completing training","Number of training modules produced","Positive employee feedback surveys"],0,"Effectiveness is shown by changed behavior and outcomes, not completion counts.","Outline 1.13",[null,"Completion rates show people attended training, not that their behavior changed.","Counting modules produced measures program output, not whether users act more securely.","Satisfaction surveys show people liked the training, which does not prove fewer risky actions."],null],["cs14",0,1,"When a supplier provides critical software components, which control BEST addresses supply chain risk?",["Rejecting all third-party software","Contractual security requirements with the right to audit and ongoing supplier assessment","Relying on the supplier's marketing materials","Scanning the supplier's website for vulnerabilities once"],1,"Supply chain risk management needs contractual requirements and continuous assessment of the supplier.","Outline 1.12",["Rejecting all third-party software is impractical for almost any organization and avoids the risk only by giving up needed capability.",null,"Marketing materials are self-promotion, not independent evidence of the supplier's security.","A one-time scan of the supplier's website says nothing about the security of the software components it delivers."],null],["cs15",0,2,"Who is ultimately responsible for determining the classification of a data set?",["The data custodian","The system administrator","The data owner","The chief information security officer"],2,"The owner, a senior business manager, is accountable for classifying and protecting the data.","Outline 2.1",["The custodian implements and maintains controls on the owner's behalf but does not decide the classification.","A system administrator typically acts as a custodian; they operate systems but are not accountable for the data's classification.",null,"The CISO sets the classification scheme and advises, but the business owner of each data set assigns its classification."],null],["cs16",0,2,"An organization will reuse solid-state drives that stored confidential data. Which method is MOST appropriate?",["Formatting the drive","Degaussing the drive","Deleting the files and emptying the recycle bin","Cryptographic erase or manufacturer secure erase"],3,"SSDs are not magnetic and wear leveling defeats overwriting; purge via secure or cryptographic erase is appropriate.","Outline 2.4",["Formatting mainly rewrites file system structures; the underlying data remains recoverable, especially on SSDs.","Degaussing works on magnetic media; SSDs store data in flash cells, so degaussing does not erase them (and would ruin a hard drive meant for reuse).","Deleting files and emptying the recycle bin only removes pointers; the data stays on the drive until overwritten.",null],null],["cs17",0,2,"What is the PRIMARY purpose of data classification?",["To apply protection proportional to the data's value and sensitivity","To reduce the number of files stored","To comply with encryption export laws","To identify which employees created the data"],0,"Classification drives consistent, cost-effective protection and handling requirements.","Outline 2.1",[null,"Reducing file counts is a side effect of retention and cleanup, not the reason for classifying data.","Export control laws concern cryptographic products, not the classification of an organization's data.","Tracking who created data is attribution or auditing; classification is about sensitivity and value."],null],["cs18",0,2,"Under GDPR terminology, a payroll company that processes employee data on behalf of a client is the:",["Data controller","Data processor","Data subject","Data owner"],1,"The processor acts on behalf of the controller, who determines purposes and means.","Outline 2.2",["The controller decides the purposes and means of processing; here that is the client whose employees' data it is.",null,"The data subject is the individual the data describes, in this case each employee.","Data owner is an organizational role for accountability, not a GDPR term for a company processing data on another's behalf."],null],["cs19",0,2,"Which role is responsible for day-to-day implementation of the protection controls specified for data?",["Data owner","Data subject","Data custodian","Auditor"],2,"Custodians (often IT) implement and maintain controls on the owner's behalf.","Outline 2.2",["The data owner is accountable and specifies the controls but does not usually implement them day to day.","The data subject is the person the data is about and has no role in operating controls.",null,"An auditor independently checks whether controls work; they do not implement or run them."],null],["cs20",0,2,"An organization adopts a NIST control baseline and removes controls that do not apply to its systems. This is called:",["Tailoring","Baselining","Benchmarking","Scoping"],3,"Scoping removes inapplicable controls; tailoring adjusts the remaining ones.","Outline 2.6",["Tailoring modifies the remaining controls, such as adjusting parameters, to fit the organization; it is not the removal of inapplicable ones.","Baselining is establishing the starting set of controls, not trimming it.","Benchmarking compares your practices or performance to an external reference; it does not remove controls from a baseline.",null],null],["cs21",0,2,"What is the BEST reason to limit data collection to only what is necessary?",["It reduces the impact of a breach and supports privacy compliance","It makes backups faster","It eliminates the need for encryption","It removes the need for classification"],0,"Data minimization lowers exposure and aligns with privacy principles.","Outline 2.3",[null,"Backup speed is a minor operational benefit, not the main reason privacy principles call for minimization.","Collecting less data does not remove the need to encrypt the sensitive data you do keep.","Whatever data is collected still has to be classified so it can be handled correctly."],null],["cs22",0,2,"Which control BEST protects sensitive data while it is being processed in memory?",["Full-disk encryption","Confidential computing using a trusted execution environment","TLS 1.3","Data retention schedules"],1,"Data in use is protected by secure enclaves or TEEs; disk encryption and TLS cover rest and transit.","Outline 2.6",["Full-disk encryption protects data at rest; once data is loaded into memory for processing it is decrypted.",null,"TLS 1.3 protects data in transit over the network, not while an application is processing it in memory.","Retention schedules decide how long data is kept; they provide no protection while data is being processed."],null],["cs23",0,2,"A company holds customer records well beyond any legal or business requirement. What is the GREATEST risk?",["Higher backup licensing costs","Slower database queries","Increased liability and exposure if the data is breached or subject to discovery","Reduced disk space for new projects"],2,"Over-retention increases legal and breach exposure; retention should follow a defined schedule.","Outline 2.4",["Backup licensing costs are a real but minor expense compared with legal and breach exposure.","Query performance is an operational nuisance, not the greatest risk of keeping unneeded personal data.",null,"Disk space is cheap and easily expanded; it is not the main risk of over-retention."],null],["cs24",0,3,"Which security model prevents a subject at the Secret level from writing information to an Unclassified object?",["Biba","Clark-Wilson","Brewer-Nash","Bell-LaPadula"],3,"Bell-LaPadula's star property (no write down) protects confidentiality.","Outline 3.2",["Biba protects integrity, so it prevents writing up to higher-integrity objects, not writing down to lower-classified ones.","Clark-Wilson enforces integrity through well-formed transactions and separation of duties, not classification levels.","Brewer-Nash prevents conflicts of interest between competing clients rather than enforcing classification levels.",null],null],["cs25",0,3,"Which model is designed to prevent conflicts of interest, such as a consultant accessing data of competing clients?",["Brewer-Nash (Chinese Wall)","Bell-LaPadula","Biba","Take-Grant"],0,"Brewer-Nash dynamically restricts access based on previously accessed data sets.","Outline 3.2",[null,"Bell-LaPadula enforces confidentiality by classification level; it does not track conflicts between competing clients.","Biba enforces integrity levels and has no notion of competing data sets.","Take-Grant models how rights are passed between subjects and objects; it is not built around conflict-of-interest classes."],null],["cs26",0,3,"Which Biba rule prevents a high-integrity process from reading low-integrity data?",["Star integrity property (no write up)","Simple integrity property (no read down)","Simple security property (no read up)","Discretionary security property"],1,"Biba's simple integrity property stops reading lower-integrity data that could corrupt the subject.","Outline 3.2",["The star integrity property prevents writing up to higher-integrity objects, not reading lower-integrity data.",null,"No read up is Bell-LaPadula's simple security property for confidentiality, not a Biba integrity rule.","The discretionary security property is Bell-LaPadula's use of an access matrix, not a Biba integrity rule."],null],["cs27",0,3,"Which cryptographic mechanism provides integrity, authentication and non-repudiation of a message?",["Symmetric encryption","Message digest alone","Digital signature","Key escrow"],2,"A signature made with the sender's private key proves origin and integrity and cannot be repudiated.","Outline 3.6",["Symmetric encryption gives confidentiality, but because both parties share the key it cannot prove which one sent a message.","A digest alone detects changes, but anyone can recompute it, so it provides no authentication or non-repudiation.",null,"Key escrow stores copies of keys with a third party for recovery; it does not protect messages."],null],["cs28",0,3,"An attacker measures power consumption of a smart card to recover its key. This is a:",["Brute-force attack","Birthday attack","Replay attack","Side-channel attack"],3,"Side-channel attacks exploit physical emissions like power, timing or electromagnetic leakage.","Outline 3.7",["Brute force tries every possible key mathematically; it does not rely on measuring the device's physical behavior.","A birthday attack exploits hash collision probability and has nothing to do with power measurements.","A replay attack resends captured valid messages; it does not recover keys from physical emissions.",null],null],["cs29",0,3,"A physical door must be designed for the case where power fails. For an emergency exit in an occupied building, what should it do?",["Fail open (fail-safe) to allow people to exit","Fail secure to protect assets","Lock and trigger an alarm","Remain in its last state"],0,"Life safety always takes priority over asset protection.","Outline 3.9",[null,"Fail secure protects assets but could trap occupants during an emergency, and life safety always comes first.","Locking the exit during a power failure puts occupants at risk; an alarm does not let them out.","Remaining in its last state could leave the door locked with people inside, which fails the life-safety requirement."],null],["cs30",0,3,"Which fire suppression system is MOST suitable for a data center to reduce accidental water discharge?",["Wet pipe sprinkler system","Pre-action sprinkler system","Deluge system","Handheld Class A extinguishers only"],1,"Pre-action requires a detection event before the pipes fill, reducing accidental discharge.","Outline 3.9",["Wet pipe systems hold water in the pipes at all times, so a damaged pipe or head discharges water immediately.",null,"Deluge systems open all heads at once and release large volumes of water, which suits high-hazard areas, not data centers.","Class A extinguishers are for ordinary combustibles and are not a fixed suppression system for a whole data center."],null],["cs31",0,3,"What is the MOST important factor in the strength of a cryptographic implementation?",["Secrecy of the algorithm","Length of the ciphertext","Protection and management of the keys","Speed of the encryption"],2,"Kerckhoffs's principle: security should rest on the key, so key management is critical.","Outline 3.6",["Relying on secret algorithms is security through obscurity; Kerckhoffs's principle says a system must stay secure even when the algorithm is public.","Ciphertext length usually tracks plaintext length and says nothing about how hard the encryption is to break.",null,"Speed affects performance, not how resistant the cryptography is to attack."],null],["cs32",0,3,"Which component provides hardware-based secure key storage and platform integrity measurements on a laptop?",["BIOS password","Hypervisor","Memory management unit","Trusted Platform Module (TPM)"],3,"The TPM stores keys and measures boot components for attestation.","Outline 3.4",["A BIOS password restricts firmware settings but provides no secure key storage or boot measurements.","A hypervisor manages virtual machines; it is software and does not provide a hardware root of trust for keys.","The MMU translates and isolates memory addresses; it does not store keys or measure boot integrity.",null],null],["cs33",0,3,"An organization runs industrial control systems that cannot be patched. What is the BEST compensating control?",["Isolating the systems in a segmented network with strict access control and monitoring","Replacing the systems immediately regardless of cost","Installing antivirus on the PLCs","Disabling all logging to improve performance"],0,"Segmentation and monitoring reduce exposure when patching is not possible.","Outline 3.5",[null,"Immediate replacement regardless of cost ignores business impact and is rarely feasible for industrial systems; a compensating control is needed now.","PLCs generally cannot run antivirus, and adding software could affect safety and vendor support.","Disabling logging removes visibility and weakens security; it does nothing to compensate for missing patches."],null],["cs34",0,3,"Which secure design principle is violated when a new application ships with all features and admin interfaces enabled?",["Separation of duties","Secure defaults","Keep it simple","Shared responsibility"],1,"Systems should ship in their most secure configuration, with features enabled only when needed.","Outline 3.1",["Separation of duties splits critical tasks between people; it concerns roles, not default configuration.",null,"Keep it simple (economy of mechanism) is about minimizing design complexity, not which features are on by default.","Shared responsibility describes the split of security duties between a cloud provider and customer."],null],["cs35",0,3,"Two users need to exchange a symmetric key over an untrusted network without prior contact. Which algorithm is designed for this?",["AES","SHA-256","Diffie-Hellman","3DES"],2,"Diffie-Hellman key agreement lets parties establish a shared secret over an insecure channel.","Outline 3.6",["AES is a symmetric cipher that needs a shared key already in place; it cannot establish one on its own.","SHA-256 is a hash function used for integrity; it does not exchange keys.",null,"3DES is a symmetric cipher that also requires a pre-shared key, and it is deprecated."],null],["cs36",0,4,"At which OSI layer does a router make forwarding decisions based on IP addresses?",["Data link layer (Layer 2)","Transport layer (Layer 4)","Session layer (Layer 5)","Network layer (Layer 3)"],3,"IP addressing and routing occur at Layer 3.","Outline 4.1",["Layer 2 devices such as switches forward frames by MAC address, not by IP address.","Layer 4 handles end-to-end transport such as TCP and UDP ports, not routing between networks.","Layer 5 manages sessions between applications and plays no part in packet forwarding.",null],null],["cs37",0,4,"Which protocol suite provides confidentiality and integrity for traffic at the network layer between two sites?",["IPsec","TLS","SSH","SNMPv2c"],0,"IPsec operates at Layer 3 and is the standard for site-to-site VPNs.","Outline 4.1",[null,"TLS protects individual application sessions at the transport layer and above, not all traffic at the network layer.","SSH secures remote sessions and tunnels at the application layer; it is not the standard for network-layer site-to-site protection.","SNMPv2c is a network management protocol with cleartext community strings and provides no confidentiality."],null],["cs38",0,4,"In IPsec, which protocol provides encryption of the payload?",["Authentication Header (AH)","Encapsulating Security Payload (ESP)","Internet Key Exchange (IKE)","Generic Routing Encapsulation (GRE)"],1,"ESP provides confidentiality; AH provides integrity and authentication only.","Outline 4.1",["AH provides integrity and origin authentication but does not encrypt the payload.",null,"IKE negotiates security associations and keys; it does not itself encrypt the data packets.","GRE is a tunneling protocol with no encryption; it is often paired with IPsec for that reason."],null],["cs39",0,4,"An organization wants to stop attackers from moving laterally between workloads in its data center. What is the BEST approach?",["Add a larger perimeter firewall","Increase internet bandwidth","Implement micro-segmentation with policy enforced per workload","Deploy a CDN"],2,"Micro-segmentation controls east-west traffic between workloads.","Outline 4.1",["A bigger perimeter firewall controls north-south traffic entering the data center but does not see east-west traffic between workloads.","More bandwidth improves capacity and does nothing to restrict lateral movement.",null,"A CDN caches and delivers public content; it does not control traffic between internal workloads."],null],["cs40",0,4,"Which wireless security configuration is MOST appropriate for a corporate network?",["WPA2-Personal with a strong passphrase","WEP with MAC filtering","An open network with a captive portal","WPA3-Enterprise with 802.1X authentication"],3,"Enterprise mode gives per-user authentication and revocation.","Outline 4.1",["WPA2-Personal uses one shared passphrase, so there is no per-user authentication and revoking one user means changing it for everyone.","WEP is cryptographically broken, and MAC addresses are easily spoofed.","An open network has no over-the-air encryption; a captive portal only gates access and does not protect traffic.",null],null],["cs41",0,4,"Which device inspects traffic inline and can automatically block detected attacks?",["Intrusion prevention system","Intrusion detection system","Network tap","Protocol analyzer"],0,"IPS sits inline and blocks; IDS only alerts.","Outline 4.2",[null,"An IDS monitors a copy of traffic and alerts, but it is not inline and cannot block attacks itself.","A network tap only copies traffic to a monitoring tool; it performs no inspection or blocking.","A protocol analyzer captures and decodes packets for troubleshooting but does not block anything."],null],["cs42",0,4,"What is the BEST mitigation for a large volumetric DDoS attack against a public website?",["Adding more RAM to the web server","Contracting an upstream DDoS scrubbing service or CDN","Blocking ICMP at the host firewall","Increasing log retention"],1,"Volumetric attacks saturate links before on-premises controls can help, so mitigation must be upstream.","Outline 4.2",["Extra RAM does not help when the attack floods the network link before traffic reaches the server.",null,"Blocking ICMP on the host does nothing once the inbound link is already saturated, and volumetric floods use many protocols.","Longer log retention helps later analysis but does not mitigate an attack in progress."],null],["cs43",0,4,"Which control verifies a device's health and identity before allowing it onto the network?",["Proxy server","Load balancer","Network access control (NAC)","DNS sinkhole"],2,"NAC checks posture and authentication before granting network access.","Outline 4.2",["A proxy mediates application requests such as web traffic; it does not check device health before network admission.","A load balancer spreads traffic across servers and does not authenticate or assess connecting devices.",null,"A DNS sinkhole redirects lookups for malicious domains; it does not check device posture or identity."],null],["cs44",0,4,"Which protocol should replace Telnet for remote administration?",["FTP","rlogin","SNMPv1","SSH"],3,"SSH encrypts the session, including credentials; Telnet sends them in cleartext.","Outline 4.3",["FTP also sends credentials in cleartext and is for file transfer, not remote administration.","rlogin is a legacy cleartext remote login protocol with weak trust-based authentication, as insecure as Telnet.","SNMPv1 is a management protocol with cleartext community strings, not an encrypted remote shell.",null],null],["cs45",0,4,"Which technology is MOST effective at protecting DNS responses from being forged?",["DNSSEC","DNS over UDP","Split-horizon DNS","Round-robin DNS"],0,"DNSSEC signs DNS records so resolvers can verify authenticity and integrity.","Outline 4.2",[null,"DNS over UDP is simply the normal transport for DNS and adds no authentication of responses.","Split-horizon DNS returns different answers to internal and external clients; it does not stop forged responses.","Round-robin DNS spreads load across several addresses and provides no integrity protection."],null],["cs46",0,4,"A company is connecting a third-party vendor to its network for support. What is the BEST approach?",["Give the vendor a standard employee VPN account","Provide restricted, monitored access to only the required systems under a signed agreement","Allow direct RDP from the internet","Share an administrator password by email"],1,"Third-party connectivity should be least privilege, contractual and monitored.","Outline 4.3",["A standard employee VPN account usually grants broad network access that a vendor does not need and is harder to monitor and revoke.",null,"Direct RDP from the internet exposes systems to brute-force and exploitation and gives no granular control.","Sharing an admin password by email exposes it in transit and storage and removes individual accountability."],null],["cs47",0,4,"Which converged protocol carries SCSI storage commands over IP networks and should be isolated on its own network?",["VoIP","SIP","iSCSI","MPLS"],2,"iSCSI carries storage traffic over IP and should be segmented and authenticated.","Outline 4.1",["VoIP carries voice traffic over IP, not SCSI storage commands.","SIP is a signaling protocol for setting up voice and video calls, not a storage protocol.",null,"MPLS is a label-switching WAN transport technology; it does not carry SCSI commands itself."],null],["cs48",0,5,"An employee transfers from finance to marketing but keeps finance system access. What is this called?",["Privilege escalation","Account takeover","Session hijacking","Privilege creep"],3,"Accumulated access from role changes is privilege creep; access reviews and mover processes prevent it.","Outline 5.5",["Privilege escalation is gaining higher rights through an exploit or misconfiguration, not keeping old rights after a job change.","Account takeover is an attacker gaining control of a legitimate account, which did not happen here.","Session hijacking is stealing an active session token; it has nothing to do with leftover access from a transfer.",null],null],["cs49",0,5,"What is the BEST control to detect inappropriate accumulated access rights?",["Periodic user access reviews by managers or data owners","Longer password requirements","Annual security awareness training","Account lockout thresholds"],0,"Recertification catches access that is no longer needed.","Outline 5.5",[null,"Longer passwords strengthen authentication but do nothing to reveal access a user no longer needs.","Awareness training changes user behavior but does not identify excessive permissions.","Lockout thresholds counter password guessing and do not reveal accumulated rights."],null],["cs50",0,5,"Which access control model assigns permissions based on job function?",["Mandatory access control (MAC)","Role-based access control (RBAC)","Discretionary access control (DAC)","Rule-based access control"],1,"RBAC ties permissions to roles that map to job functions.","Outline 5.4",["MAC grants access by comparing system-enforced labels and clearances, not by job function.",null,"DAC lets data owners grant access at their own discretion rather than by role.","Rule-based access control applies global rules, such as firewall rules or time-of-day limits, to all users regardless of job."],null],["cs51",0,5,"Which model uses security labels and clearances enforced by the system rather than the data owner?",["Discretionary access control (DAC)","Role-based access control (RBAC)","Mandatory access control (MAC)","Attribute-based access control (ABAC)"],2,"In MAC the system enforces label-based decisions; users cannot change them.","Outline 5.4",["In DAC the data owner decides who gets access, which is the opposite of system-enforced labels.","RBAC assigns permissions through roles tied to job functions, not through classification labels and clearances.",null,"ABAC evaluates policies over many attributes; it is not the classic label-and-clearance model the question describes."],null],["cs52",0,5,"A user logs in once and gains access to several web applications from different organizations using SAML. What is this?",["Kerberos realm trust","Password synchronization","Local account mapping","Federated identity"],3,"Federation lets identity from one organization's IdP be trusted by another's service provider.","Outline 5.3",["Kerberos realm trusts link Kerberos domains, typically within an enterprise; they do not use SAML across organizations' web apps.","Password synchronization keeps the same password on several systems, but the user still logs in to each one separately.","Local account mapping ties accounts together within one system and does not give cross-organization single sign-on.",null],null],["cs53",0,5,"Which protocol is an identity layer built on OAuth 2.0?",["OpenID Connect","SAML 2.0","LDAP","RADIUS"],0,"OIDC adds authentication (ID tokens) on top of OAuth 2.0 authorization.","Outline 5.3",[null,"SAML 2.0 is an XML-based federation standard that is separate from OAuth 2.0, not built on it.","LDAP is a directory access protocol, not an identity layer on top of OAuth.","RADIUS is a network AAA protocol for things like VPN and Wi-Fi access and is unrelated to OAuth."],null],["cs54",0,5,"In Kerberos, what does the Key Distribution Center issue after initial authentication?",["A SAML assertion","A ticket-granting ticket","An X.509 certificate","A one-time password"],1,"The authentication service issues a TGT used to request service tickets.","Outline 5.6",["SAML assertions come from a SAML identity provider in web federation, not from a Kerberos KDC.",null,"X.509 certificates are issued by a certificate authority in a PKI, not by the Kerberos KDC.","Kerberos does not issue one-time passwords; OTPs come from token devices or authenticator apps."],null],["cs55",0,5,"When comparing biometric systems, which metric is the BEST single indicator of accuracy?",["False acceptance rate alone","Enrollment time","Crossover error rate (CER)","Throughput rate"],2,"CER is where FAR equals FRR; lower is more accurate.","Outline 5.6",["FAR alone ignores false rejections, so a system can have a low FAR simply by rejecting almost everyone.","Enrollment time affects user acceptance, not how accurately the system matches people.",null,"Throughput is how many people the system can process per minute, a speed measure rather than accuracy."],null],["cs56",0,5,"What is the MOST important step when an employee is terminated for cause?",["Delete the user's mailbox immediately","Collect the badge a week later","Send a company-wide announcement","Disable the user's access at or before the time they are notified"],3,"Access removal must be timely to prevent retaliation; data may need to be retained.","Outline 5.5",["Deleting the mailbox immediately may destroy evidence or records that must be retained; access should be disabled, not the data erased.","Waiting a week to collect the badge leaves physical access open to a possibly disgruntled former employee.","A company-wide announcement is not a priority and can raise privacy or legal concerns; removing access is what protects the organization.",null],null],["cs57",0,5,"Administrators should hold elevated rights only when approved and for a limited time. Which approach does this describe?",["Just-in-time privileged access","Single sign-on","Discretionary access control","Password vaulting without checkout"],0,"JIT removes standing privilege, reducing attack surface.","Outline 5.2",[null,"Single sign-on lets users authenticate once to many systems; it does not limit how long elevated rights last.","DAC lets owners grant access at their discretion and does not provide time-bound, approved elevation.","A vault without checkout leaves standing access to privileged credentials, the opposite of time-limited approval."],null],["cs58",0,5,"Which combination is true multifactor authentication?",["A password and a security question","A password and a hardware token code","A fingerprint and a palm scan","A PIN and a passphrase"],1,"MFA requires different factor types: something you know plus something you have.","Outline 5.2",["A password and a security question are both something you know, so this is single-factor authentication.",null,"A fingerprint and a palm scan are both something you are, so they are the same factor type.","A PIN and a passphrase are both something you know, so this is still single-factor."],null],["cs59",0,5,"Which centralized AAA protocol encrypts the entire payload and separates authentication, authorization and accounting?",["RADIUS","PAP","TACACS+","LDAP"],2,"TACACS+ uses TCP, encrypts the full body and separates AAA functions; RADIUS encrypts only the password.","Outline 5.6",["RADIUS uses UDP, encrypts only the password field and combines authentication with authorization.","PAP sends credentials in cleartext and is only an authentication method, not a centralized AAA protocol.",null,"LDAP is a directory protocol; it does not provide separate accounting or full-payload encryption as an AAA service."],null],["cs60",0,6,"What must be obtained BEFORE beginning a penetration test?",["A list of known vulnerabilities","Administrator passwords for all systems","Approval from the internet service provider","Written authorization from management with authority over the systems"],3,"Testing without written permission may be illegal and violates ethics.","Outline 6.2",["A list of known vulnerabilities may help scoping, but testing without permission is illegal even with that list in hand.","Testers do not need every admin password up front, and credentials do not replace legal authorization.","The ISP does not own the target systems; authorization must come from management who own them.",null],null],["cs61",0,6,"A customer wants assurance that a vendor's controls operated effectively over the past year. Which report should they request?",["SOC 2 Type II","SOC 2 Type I","SOC 1 Type I","SOC 3"],0,"Type II tests operating effectiveness over a period; SOC 2 covers security controls.","Outline 6.5",[null,"SOC 2 Type I assesses the design of controls at a single point in time, not how they operated over the past year.","SOC 1 covers controls relevant to financial reporting, and Type I is only a point-in-time design review.","SOC 3 is a short general-use summary without the detailed test results a customer needs to evaluate operating effectiveness."],null],["cs62",0,6,"Which technique proactively tests an application's availability by running scripted user actions?",["Real user monitoring","Synthetic transactions","Log review","Static code analysis"],1,"Synthetic monitoring simulates users to detect issues before real users are affected.","Outline 6.2",["Real user monitoring passively observes actual user traffic; it does not proactively run scripted actions.",null,"Log review is after-the-fact analysis of recorded events, not proactive testing of availability.","Static analysis examines source code without running the application, so it cannot test availability."],null],["cs63",0,6,"Which testing technique sends malformed or random input to find crashes and unexpected behavior?",["Static analysis","Code review","Fuzzing","Regression testing"],2,"Fuzz testing uses invalid or random input to find robustness issues.","Outline 6.2",["Static analysis inspects code without executing it and does not feed input to a running program.","Code review is a manual inspection of source code, not automated input testing.",null,"Regression testing re-runs existing tests to confirm changes did not break features; it does not generate malformed input."],null],["cs64",0,6,"A penetration tester is given full network diagrams and source code. What type of test is this?",["Black box (zero knowledge)","Gray box (partial knowledge)","Blind test","White box (full knowledge)"],3,"White-box testers have complete information about the target.","Outline 6.2",["A black box tester has no prior knowledge of the target, the opposite of receiving diagrams and code.","A gray box tester gets only partial information, such as user credentials, not full diagrams and source code.","A blind test gives the tester little or no information about the target, though the defenders know a test is happening.",null],null],["cs65",0,6,"What is the PRIMARY purpose of an independent third-party audit?",["To provide objective assurance to stakeholders about controls","To fix vulnerabilities found internally","To replace internal testing","To train the security team"],0,"Independence gives objectivity and credibility to the findings.","Outline 6.1",[null,"Auditors assess and report; fixing vulnerabilities is management's responsibility, and auditors fixing issues would undermine their independence.","External audits complement internal testing but do not replace ongoing internal assessment.","Training the security team is not the purpose of an audit, which is to give stakeholders objective assurance."],null],["cs66",0,6,"After a vulnerability assessment, what should the security manager do FIRST with the results?",["Patch every finding immediately","Prioritize findings by risk to the business and assign remediation owners","Share the raw scan output with all staff","Rerun the scan with more plugins"],1,"Results must be analyzed and risk-ranked so resources go to the most important issues.","Outline 6.4",["Patching every finding immediately ignores risk ranking, wastes effort on low-risk items and can cause outages without testing.",null,"Raw scan output is sensitive, since it maps weaknesses, and should be shared only with people who need it.","Rerunning with more plugins produces more data but does not act on the findings already in hand."],null],["cs67",0,6,"Which metric is a key risk indicator rather than a key performance indicator?",["Number of training sessions delivered","Help desk tickets closed per week","Number of critical vulnerabilities older than 30 days","Firewall uptime percentage"],2,"KRIs signal rising risk exposure; aging critical vulnerabilities indicate growing risk.","Outline 6.3",["Training sessions delivered measures program activity, which is a performance indicator, not a signal of rising risk.","Tickets closed per week measures help desk productivity, a performance indicator.",null,"Firewall uptime measures service availability, a performance indicator rather than a sign of growing risk exposure."],null],["cs68",0,6,"Why should backup restoration be tested regularly?",["To reduce storage costs","To satisfy antivirus requirements","To remove old backup media","To verify data can actually be recovered within the required objectives"],3,"Untested backups may fail when needed; restore tests prove RTO and RPO can be met.","Outline 6.3",["Restore testing uses extra resources; it is not a way to reduce storage costs.","Antivirus has no requirement for backup restore tests.","Retiring old media is part of media management; restore tests verify recoverability, not disposal.",null],null],["cs69",0,6,"Which type of scan gives the MOST accurate view of missing patches on a server?",["Credentialed (authenticated) scan","Unauthenticated external scan","Ping sweep","Banner grab"],0,"Authenticated scans can inspect installed software and configurations directly.","Outline 6.2",[null,"An unauthenticated external scan can only infer patch levels from exposed services and banners, which is far less accurate.","A ping sweep only finds live hosts and reveals nothing about installed patches.","Banner grabbing reads service version strings, which can be misleading or suppressed and do not show OS or back-ported patches."],null],["cs70",0,7,"During an incident, what is the FIRST priority?",["Preserving evidence","Ensuring the safety of people","Restoring operations","Notifying the media"],1,"Human life and safety always come first.","Outline 7.15",["Preserving evidence is important but always comes after protecting human life and safety.",null,"Restoring operations is a later recovery goal and never outranks the safety of people.","Media notification is handled later through the communications plan, if needed at all."],null],["cs71",0,7,"What is the PRIMARY purpose of chain of custody documentation?",["To speed up forensic analysis","To identify the attacker","To prove evidence has been handled properly and not altered","To reduce storage requirements"],2,"Chain of custody establishes integrity and admissibility of evidence.","Outline 7.1",["Chain of custody records who handled evidence and when; it does not make analysis faster.","Identifying the attacker is the goal of the investigation, not of the custody record.",null,"Chain of custody has nothing to do with storage efficiency."],null],["cs72",0,7,"Which order of volatility should a forensic investigator follow when collecting evidence?",["Disk, RAM, archived media, CPU cache","Archived media, disk, RAM, CPU cache","RAM, archived media, disk, CPU cache","CPU cache and registers, RAM, disk, archived media"],3,"Collect the most volatile data first because it is lost soonest.","Outline 7.1",["This starts with disk, the least volatile live source, so RAM and cache would be lost first.","This is exactly reversed: archived media is the least volatile source and should be collected last.","RAM should come early, but CPU cache is more volatile and is collected first, and archived media belongs at the end.",null],null],["cs73",0,7,"Which recovery site provides the fastest recovery but at the highest cost?",["Hot site","Warm site","Cold site","Mobile site"],0,"Hot sites are fully equipped with current data and can take over quickly.","Outline 7.10",[null,"A warm site has some equipment but needs data restoration and setup, so recovery is slower and cheaper than a hot site.","A cold site has only space, power and connectivity, so it is the cheapest and slowest option.","A mobile site is a transportable facility that must be deployed and configured, so it is not the fastest recovery option."],null],["cs74",0,7,"Which DR test runs the recovery site's systems while the primary site continues normal operation?",["Full interruption test","Parallel test","Read-through test","Walkthrough test"],1,"Parallel tests process real work at the alternate site without shutting down production.","Outline 7.12",["A full interruption test shuts down the primary site and shifts all work to the recovery site, the riskiest test type.",null,"A read-through (checklist) test only has participants review the plan documents; no systems are run.","A walkthrough is a group discussion of the plan's steps; no recovery systems process real work."],null],["cs75",0,7,"Which backup type copies all changes since the last full backup and does NOT reset the archive bit?",["Incremental","Full","Differential","Copy-on-write snapshot"],2,"Differential backups grow until the next full backup; restore needs the full plus the latest differential.","Outline 7.10",["An incremental backup copies changes since the last backup of any kind and does reset the archive bit.","A full backup copies everything, not just changes, and resets the archive bit.",null,"A copy-on-write snapshot is a point-in-time image mechanism, not an archive-bit-based backup type."],null],["cs76",0,7,"Job rotation is MOST effective at which of the following?",["Improving system performance","Reducing licensing costs","Preventing malware infections","Detecting fraud and collusion"],3,"Another person performing the role may uncover irregularities.","Outline 7.4",["Job rotation does not affect system performance; it is a personnel control.","Rotation does not reduce licensing costs and may even require more trained staff.","Job rotation does nothing to block malware; that needs technical controls.",null],null],["cs77",0,7,"A critical patch is released for a production database. What should the security manager do FIRST?",["Test the patch and apply it through the change management process","Install it immediately on all production servers","Wait for the next annual maintenance window","Disable the database until the vendor confirms stability"],0,"Patches should be tested and approved to avoid outages, with urgency matched to risk.","Outline 7.8",[null,"Installing an untested patch on all production servers risks outages and bypasses change control.","Waiting for an annual window leaves a critical vulnerability exposed far too long.","Disabling the database causes a self-inflicted outage; the risk should be handled through tested change management instead."],null],["cs78",0,7,"What is the MOST important output of the lessons-learned phase of incident response?",["A list of employees to discipline","Improvements to plans, controls and training to prevent recurrence","Deleting logs from the incident","A press release"],1,"Lessons learned exists to improve the program.","Outline 7.6",["Lessons learned is a blameless process to improve the program, not to single out employees for discipline.",null,"Incident logs must be retained as evidence and for analysis, not deleted.","Public communication is handled by the communications plan and is not the purpose of lessons learned."],null],["cs79",0,7,"Which tool automates response actions across security products based on playbooks?",["SIEM without automation","NetFlow collector","SOAR","Vulnerability scanner"],2,"SOAR orchestrates and automates response workflows.","Outline 7.2",["A SIEM without automation collects and correlates events but relies on analysts to take response actions.","A NetFlow collector records traffic metadata and does not run response playbooks.",null,"A vulnerability scanner finds weaknesses; it does not orchestrate response across security tools."],null],["cs80",0,7,"An employee traveling abroad is forced to open a secure facility under threat. Which control lets them signal this covertly?",["A mantrap","Security awareness training","Badge readers with PINs","A duress code or alarm"],3,"Duress signals alert security silently while appearing to comply.","Outline 7.15",["A mantrap controls entry to prevent tailgating but gives no way to signal distress covertly.","Awareness training teaches people what to do but is not a mechanism to send a silent signal.","A badge-and-PIN reader adds authentication, but a normal PIN gives no covert alert unless a separate duress code is configured.",null],null],["cs81",0,7,"Which is the BEST way to keep server configurations from drifting from approved settings?",["Automated configuration management against a documented baseline","Manual checks once a year","Allowing administrators to change settings as needed","Rebuilding servers only after incidents"],0,"Baselines with automated enforcement detect and correct drift.","Outline 7.3",[null,"Yearly manual checks leave drift undetected for months and are error-prone.","Letting administrators change settings freely is the cause of drift, not a control against it.","Rebuilding only after incidents is reactive and lets drift accumulate until something goes wrong."],null],["cs82",0,8,"When is the MOST cost-effective time to address security in the software development lifecycle?",["During user acceptance testing","During requirements and design","After deployment to production","During decommissioning"],1,"Defects found early are much cheaper to fix than later in the lifecycle.","Outline 8.1",["Fixing security flaws during user acceptance testing means reworking code that is already built, which costs far more than designing it in.",null,"Fixing flaws after deployment is the most expensive point, adding outages, patching and possible breach costs.","Decommissioning is the end of the lifecycle; security gaps have been exposed the whole time by then."],null],["cs83",0,8,"What is the BEST defense against SQL injection?",["Web application firewall only","Encrypting the database","Parameterized queries (prepared statements)","Hiding error messages"],2,"Parameterized queries keep data separate from code; WAFs are compensating controls.","Outline 8.5",["A WAF can filter many attack patterns but can be bypassed, so it is a compensating control, not a fix for vulnerable code.","Encrypting the database protects stored data but does not stop injected SQL from running through the application.",null,"Hiding error messages makes injection harder to discover but leaves the vulnerability in place, as blind SQL injection shows."],null],["cs84",0,8,"Which testing approach analyzes source code without executing it?",["Dynamic application security testing (DAST)","Penetration testing","Load testing","Static application security testing (SAST)"],3,"SAST reviews code at rest; DAST tests the running application.","Outline 8.2",["DAST tests the running application from the outside, so it executes the code.","Penetration testing attacks the running system and does not analyze source code at rest.","Load testing measures performance under heavy traffic by running the application, not by reviewing code.",null],null],["cs85",0,8,"An organization wants to find known-vulnerable open-source libraries in its applications. Which tool is BEST?",["Software composition analysis (SCA)","DAST scanner","Network vulnerability scanner","Fuzzer"],0,"SCA inventories third-party components and flags known vulnerabilities and license issues.","Outline 8.2",[null,"A DAST scanner tests a running app's behavior and does not inventory library versions against known CVEs.","A network vulnerability scanner checks hosts and services, not the open-source dependencies inside application code.","A fuzzer finds unknown crashes through malformed input rather than identifying known-vulnerable libraries."],null],["cs86",0,8,"Which attack tricks an authenticated user's browser into submitting an unwanted request to a trusted site?",["Cross-site scripting (XSS)","Cross-site request forgery (CSRF)","SQL injection","Buffer overflow"],1,"CSRF abuses the site's trust in the user's browser; anti-CSRF tokens mitigate it.","Outline 8.5",["XSS injects malicious script into pages viewed by users; it abuses the user's trust in the site rather than making the browser send forged requests.",null,"SQL injection targets the database through unsanitized input and does not rely on a victim's authenticated browser.","A buffer overflow writes past memory bounds in a program; it is not a browser-based forged request."],null],["cs87",0,8,"Before buying a commercial SaaS application for sensitive data, what should the security manager do FIRST?",["Run a penetration test on the vendor's production systems","Ask developers to review the vendor's source code","Assess the vendor's security posture and contractual commitments","Deploy the application to production to evaluate it"],2,"Acquired software risk is managed through vendor assessment and contracts; testing a vendor's systems needs their permission.","Outline 8.4",["Pen testing a vendor's production systems without their permission may be illegal and breach terms of service.","Commercial SaaS vendors rarely share source code, and reviewing it is not the first step in assessing acquired software.",null,"Putting sensitive data in an unvetted application in production accepts the risk before assessing it."],null],["cs88",0,8,"Why should developers NOT have access to deploy code directly into production?",["It improves code performance","It reduces the need for testing","It is required for agile methodologies","It enforces separation of duties and change control"],3,"Separating development from production prevents unauthorized or untested changes.","Outline 8.1",["Access controls on deployment have nothing to do with how fast the code runs.","Separating deployment from development does not reduce testing; it helps ensure testing actually happens.","Agile does not require developers to deploy directly to production; DevOps pipelines still enforce controlled releases.",null],null],["cs89",0,8,"Which maturity model is specifically designed to assess and improve an organization's software security practices?",["OWASP SAMM","ITIL","COBIT","ISO 9001"],0,"SAMM measures software assurance maturity and guides improvement.","Outline 8.1",[null,"ITIL is a framework for IT service management, not software security maturity.","COBIT is a governance and management framework for enterprise IT, not specific to software security practices.","ISO 9001 is a general quality management standard and does not address software security."],null],["cs90",0,8,"What is the BEST way to integrate security testing into a DevSecOps pipeline?",["Run a manual penetration test before each commit","Automate security checks as gates in the CI/CD pipeline","Test security only annually","Rely on production monitoring to find flaws"],1,"Automated gates give fast, consistent feedback without slowing delivery.","Outline 8.3",["Manual pen testing before every commit is far too slow and costly to scale in a CI/CD pipeline.",null,"Annual testing lets flaws reach production and linger for months, which defeats continuous delivery.","Relying on production monitoring finds flaws only after they are deployed and possibly exploited."],null]]);
